commons probe

// #1/#3 review: `lower_bin_op`'s general path lowered both `&&`/`||`/
// `implies` operands into the *same* caller-provided statement sink
// unconditionally, so a hoist from the right-hand operand (here,
// `helper()`'s call, forced into a temp because an `is`-receiver on a
// non-simple expression always hoists) ran even when the left operand was
// `false` and the operator's own short-circuit evaluation would otherwise
// have skipped it entirely. `design/bynk-type-system.md` documents this as
// both a performance and a safety property callers may rely on. The
// right-hand operand's hoist is now wrapped in an IIFE so it only runs when
// the operator actually reaches it.
type Outcome =
  | Hit(score: Int)
  | Miss

fn helper() -> Outcome {
  Hit(1)
}

fn check(x: Bool) -> Bool {
  x && helper() is Hit(_)
}
